1
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:08,140
Every business that invoices clients has the same quiet leak.

2
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:11,140
An invoice goes out and then life happens.

3
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:14,140
The client gets busy. The email sinks.

4
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:16,140
30 days pass, then 45.

5
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:18,140
You know the money is out there.

6
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:21,140
But chasing it means opening the tracker.

7
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:23,140
Checking the date.

8
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:25,140
Writing a polite email.

9
00:00:25,140 --> 00:00:28,140
And remembering to follow up again next week.

10
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:29,140
So it waits.

11
00:00:29,140 --> 00:00:31,140
And the longer it waits,

12
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:33,140
the more awkward it gets

13
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:36,140
and the more likely it never gets asked for at all.

14
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:38,140
The work is already done.

15
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:40,140
The invoice is already sent.

16
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:43,140
The only thing missing is the follow through.

17
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:45,140
Worry not, I'm here to close your loop.

18
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:47,140
Here's how it runs now.

19
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:49,140
Every morning, on a schedule,

20
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:53,140
the workflow opens your invoice tracker in Google Sheets.

21
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:58,140
It reads every row and works out which invoices are actually overdue.

22
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:00,140
And by how many days?

23
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:02,140
And not-due-yet invoices get skipped.

24
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:04,140
Only the real ones move forward.

25
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:06,140
Then it writes the reminder.

26
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:08,140
An AI model reads the invoice details

27
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:10,140
and drafts an email in a tone

28
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,140
that matches how overdue it is.

29
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:14,140
Gentle at 7 days.

30
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:16,140
Firm at 30 days.

31
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:18,140
It doesn't send anything.

32
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:21,140
It files the message as a Gmail draft.

33
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:23,140
With the client, the invoice number.

34
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:25,140
And the amount already filled in.

35
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:27,140
A second check validates the draft

36
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,140
before it's saved.

37
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:32,140
So a malformed email never reaches your outbox.

38
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:35,140
Then it writes the follow-up date back to the tracker.

39
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:38,140
So nobody gets chased twice in the same week.

40
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:41,140
You open Gmail, read three or four drafts,

41
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:43,140
and press send.

42
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:45,140
That's the whole job.

43
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:47,140
The chase list runs itself.

44
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:49,140
The judgement stays with you.

45
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:51,140
Stop losing invoices to silence.

46
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:53,140
Bring your tracker and your payment terms.

47
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:55,140
And I'll show you exactly

48
00:01:55,140 --> 00:01:57,140
what your overdue list is costing you.

49
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:00,140
I'm Christian and ANN Automation Architect.

50
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:02,140
Book a discovery call now.
